  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simplify the given product expression step by step. Let's start with the general form of the product:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log_c(b)}{\\log_c(a)}\\). Applying this to our expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(k)}}{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-4})}{\\log(k+1)}} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1}) \\cdot \\log(k+1)}{\\log(5^{k^2-4}) \\cdot \\log(k)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the property of logarithms that \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\). Applying this, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2-1) \\log(5) \\cdot \\log(k+1)}{(k^2-4) \\log(5) \\cdot \\log(k)} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2-1) \\log(k+1)}{(k^2-4) \\log(k)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{(k^2-1)}{(k^2-4)}\\). Notice that \\(k^2-1 = (k-1)(k+1)\\) and \\(k^2-4 = (k-2)(k+2)\\). So, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1) \\log(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2) \\log(k)}\n\\]\n\nWe can split this product into two separate products:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} \\cdot \\frac{k+1}{k+2} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log(k)}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's look at the first part of the product \\(\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2}\\). This is a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdots \\frac{63}{62} = \\frac{63}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's look at the second part of the product \\(\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2}\\). This is also a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\frac{7}{8} \\cdots \\frac{64}{65} = \\frac{5}{65} = \\frac{1}{13}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we have the remaining product \\(\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log(k)}\\). This is simply a product of fractions where the numerator and denominator cancel out in a telescoping manner:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log(5)}{\\log(4)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(6)}{\\log(5)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(7)}{\\log(6)} \\cdots \\frac{\\log(64)}{\\log(63)} = \\frac{\\log(64)}{\\log(4)} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{63}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{13} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{63 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 13} = \\frac{189}{26}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(189\\) and \\(26\\) are relatively prime, we have \\(m = 189\\) and \\(n = 26\\). Therefore, \\(m + n = 189 + 26 = 215\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{215}\n\\]",
